- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,300 words): a popular-science telling. It starts from the snake-in-the-box game, builds rows, columns, turns and combs, then covers the two halves of the result: long snakes are forced to turn, but averaging always finds a view with few turns, at no cost in length. It ends with the "true but cheap" verdict, the record data and the four open conjectures. Every statement is spelled out inline with `$`/`$$` math.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 3,500 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** (amsart class, same content): abstract, definitions, and statements with proof sketches of:
  - the Comb Theorem: a row sequence visiting \(r\) rows makes at least \(r-1\) turns, with equality exactly for combs;
  - step accounting: row turns plus column turns equals the length;
  - the Block Lemma: each block of constant row is a snake of the column factor;
  - the lower bound \(L+1\le(t+1)(S+1)\);
  - the hypercube splitting \(Q_{a+b}\cong Q_a\square Q_b\), giving \(L+1\le(t+1)2^b\);
  - the averaging normal form \(\frac{L+1}{2^{n-a}}-1\le t\le \frac{aL}{n}\), reached by a relabelling that keeps the length.

  The paper also has three algorithms, a discussion of why the conjecture holds but carries little information, a data table and the four conjectures.
